MERIDIAN — Evidence System

Dossier: Research / Investigation
ID: 08
Status: Foundation

---

MERIDIAN needs to know the difference between something being found, something being understood, and something being believed.

These are not the same.

A historical source may provide evidence for one claim and no evidence for another. An interpretation may be convincing without being established fact. A design decision may be intelligent without having a historical precedent.

The Evidence System exists to keep these distinctions visible.

---

The basic chain

Every significant research claim should be traceable through a chain:

"SOURCE → EVIDENCE → CLAIM → INTERPRETATION → QUESTION → HYPOTHESIS → DECISION → TRANSFORMATION → RESULT"

Not every research unit will contain every step.

The purpose of the chain is not bureaucracy.

It is to make it possible to look at an architectural result and ask:

Where did this come from?

And then move backwards until the answer reaches something that can actually be examined.

---

Evidence is not the same as truth

Evidence supports a claim.

It does not automatically make the claim true.

The strength of a claim depends on the quality, relevance, context, and interpretation of the evidence behind it.

This is particularly important in architectural history, where drawings, buildings, archival records, photographs, scholarly interpretations, and later reconstructions may provide different kinds of knowledge.

MERIDIAN therefore does not use a source as a shortcut to certainty.

---

Epistemic states

MERIDIAN uses the following states to distinguish different kinds of knowledge:

F1 — Established Fact

A claim directly supported by reliable evidence.

F2 — Scholarly Consensus

A position strongly established across credible scholarship.

F3 — Plausible Scholarly Interpretation

An interpretation supported by serious scholarship or evidence, but not sufficiently established to be treated as fact.

I1 — Authorial Interpretation

An interpretation developed by the project.

It may be strong, useful, or original, but it remains an interpretation.

H1 — Working Hypothesis

A proposition being investigated or tested.

It is allowed to fail.

U1 — Unknown

The available research does not currently allow a responsible conclusion.

R1 — Rejected / Disputed

A claim, interpretation, or path that has been rejected, contradicted, or shown to be unreliable.

X1 — Experimental Proposition

A proposition generated through design or research experimentation whose validity is not yet established.

D1 — Design Decision

A decision made by the project.

It does not require a historical source merely because it affects architecture, but its reasoning should be recoverable when it is consequential.

---

Source classes

The system distinguishes between different kinds of sources because they do not carry the same evidentiary role.

S1 — Primary Architectural Sources
Buildings, original drawings, inscriptions, archival documents, contemporary records, and other direct historical material.

S2 — Scholarly Sources
Peer-reviewed research, academic books, specialist studies, and established scholarly work.

S3 — Institutional Sources
Museums, archives, heritage organizations, universities, governmental or other authoritative institutions.

S4 — Secondary Interpretations
Critical essays, later interpretations, reviews, and other mediated accounts.

S5 — Visual / Documentary Sources
Photographs, measured drawings, maps, visual records, documentary material, and comparable evidence.

S6 — Project-Generated Evidence
MERIDIAN's own drawings, models, experiments, observations, tests, failures, and architectural results.

The source class does not determine the truth of a claim by itself.

It determines what kind of evidence is being used.

---

The separation that must remain

MERIDIAN should never silently move between these statements:

“The source shows this.”

“This can be interpreted as this.”

“The project extracts this principle.”

“The project proposes this transformation.”

“The resulting architecture demonstrates this.”

Each statement belongs to a different epistemic position.

The system becomes unreliable when they are compressed into one sentence.

---

Evidence strength and design relevance

Two different questions must be kept apart:

How well is this claim supported?

and

How useful is this claim for MERIDIAN?

A historically weak idea may generate an interesting design experiment, but it should not become historically authoritative because the design is successful.

Likewise, a strongly established historical fact may have little transformation potential.

Evidence quality and design usefulness are therefore separate dimensions.

This distinction prevents the project from selecting history merely because it supports the desired architecture.

---

Negative evidence

Evidence is not only what confirms a direction.

Contradictions, failed interpretations, incompatible sources, unsuccessful transformations, and missing information can be equally important.

A rejected interpretation should not simply disappear when it has affected the development of the project.

Where useful, MERIDIAN should preserve:

"what was believed → what challenged it → what changed"

This creates a record of intellectual development rather than a polished reconstruction of success.

---

The minimum record

A significant research unit should be identifiable through:

- ID
- Source
- Source class
- Claim
- Evidence
- Epistemic state
- Interpretation, where applicable
- Related question
- Extraction, where applicable
- Design implication, where applicable
- Confidence / uncertainty
- Date / version

Not every field needs to be populated every time.

The record should be as small as possible while remaining capable of preserving the reasoning.

---

The integrity rule

When evidence is insufficient, MERIDIAN should lower the certainty of the statement before increasing the certainty of the architecture.

When interpretation is strong but unproven, it should remain interpretation.

When a hypothesis fails, it should be allowed to fail.

When a design decision is authorial, it does not need to be disguised as historical necessity.

The project becomes stronger when these distinctions are visible.

---

Why this system exists

The purpose of evidence is not to make MERIDIAN look academic.

It is to protect the project from its own ability to produce convincing explanations.

A beautiful transformation can make its origin seem obvious after the fact.

A coherent narrative can make an uncertain historical reading appear established.

A well-designed diagram can make a hypothesis look like evidence.

The Evidence System is the barrier against that drift.

Its standard is simple:

If a claim matters, its position in the chain should be recoverable.

And if it cannot yet be recovered, MERIDIAN should say so.
